/// `second-order-rules`: Second-order rules.
/// Implemented: a rule over a binding operation, instantiation `t(u)`, the abstraction `bind x . t` at a port, sorted at its binders, oriented by HORPO's metavariable case, its critical pairs joined in the second-order reading, and an inspected call fired in that reading.
/// Not implemented: two binders at one port, which waits on the grammar; completing a parting pair (#2361); a law relating a term at its port (#1324); #2347 owes it.
// Second-order rules. A rule whose right side applies a variable that a
// binding port of its left side binds instantiates it: `t(u)` substitutes `u`
// for the variable `t`'s port binds, one argument per bound variable (Klop,
// van Oostrom and van Raamsdonk, _Combinatory reduction systems: introduction
// and survey_, TCS 121, 1993). The rules terminate by the higher-order
// recursive path order (Jouannaud and Rubio, _The higher-order recursive path
// ordering_, LICS 1999): the instantiation is below an application that holds
// the variable and every argument directly. A pair a second-order rule takes
// part in is found by pattern unification and joined in the second-order
// reading (Mayr and Nipkow, _Higher-order rewrite systems and their
// confluence_, TCS 192, 1998).

/// ζ: `let` binds a term over its body, and its rule substitutes the bound
/// term into the body.
@[ checks ]
theory Let {
  sort Tm : Type+;
  oper let : (u : Tm, t : pi+ (x : Tm) . Tm) --> Tm;
  rule zeta : let(u, t) <=> t(u);
}

/// A body binding two terms is instantiated at two arguments, outermost
/// binder first.
@[ checks ]
theory LetPair {
  sort Tm : Type+;
  oper let2 : (u : Tm, v : Tm, t : pi+ (x : Tm) . pi+ (y : Tm) . Tm) --> Tm;
  rule zeta2 : let2(u, v, t) <=> t(u, v);
}

/// ζ beside first-order rules whose pair parts: completion joins `f`'s two
/// rules as it would without ζ, which overlaps neither.
@[ checks ]
theory LetBeside {
  sort Tm : Type+;
  oper zero : () --> Tm;
  oper one : () --> Tm;
  oper f : (a : Tm) --> Tm;
  oper let : (u : Tm, t : pi+ (x : Tm) . Tm) --> Tm;
  rule zeta : let(u, t) <=> t(u);
  rule fZero : f(a) <=> zero();
  rule fOne : f(a) <=> one();
}

/// ζ beside a rule rewriting `let` at `zero()`: their left sides overlap at
/// the root, both reducts are `t(zero())` (#2328), and an inspected call
/// fires both by substitution, the inner `let` at `x` and the outer at
/// `zero()` (#2966).
@[ normalizes("let(zero, bind x . let(x, bind y . y)) ~> zero") ]
theory LetOverlap {
  sort Tm : Type+;
  oper zero : () --> Tm;
  oper let : (u : Tm, t : pi+ (x : Tm) . Tm) --> Tm;
  rule zeta : let(u, t) <=> t(u);
  rule zetaZero : let(zero(), t) <=> t(zero());
}

/// ζ's right side is the instantiation of `t`, which the presentation keeps
/// as an `Inst` node.
@[ runs("1") ]
def instantiates() -> Integer { case Let {
  Presents(sorts, opers, rules) => case rules {
    Rule(name, left, right, rest) => case right {
      Inst(variable, arguments) => 1,
      _ => 0
    },
    _ => 0
  },
  _ => 0
} }
